  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents powered by large language models (LLMs) has revolutionized software development workflows, enabling automated code generation, execution, and debugging. However, the increasing integration of these agents into critical systems raises significant security and safety concerns. This paper reviews recent research advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies designed to identify and mitigate vulnerabilities in these systems. We examine three key areas: (1) automatic red-teaming agents that systematically probe code agents for vulnerabilities, (2) benchmarks for evaluating the safety of risky code execution and generation, and (3) blue teaming approaches that leverage red-teaming insights to improve defensive capabilities. Our analysis reveals that while significant progress has been made in understanding the trustworthiness of LLM-based systems, substantial challenges remain in developing robust defense mechanisms for code agents. We identify key research gaps and propose directions for future work in this critical area of AI safety.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in how software is developed, maintained, and deployed. By integrating large language models with code interpreters and development tools, these agents can autonomously write, test, and execute code across a wide range of tasks. From simple script generation to complex software architecture design, code agents are increasingly being deployed in production environments.\n\nHowever, this powerful capability introduces new security challenges. Code agents execute arbitrary code, which means that if they are prompted or manipulated to generate malicious code, the consequences can be severe. Vulnerabilities in code agents can lead to:\n\n1. **Code injection attacks**: Malicious prompts that cause the agent to generate harmful code\n2. **Remote code execution**: Agents executing untrusted or malicious code in their runtime environments\n3. **Data exfiltration**: Agents leaking sensitive information through generated code\n4. **Supply chain attacks**: Compromised dependencies or packages introduced through agent-generated code\n\nThe field of red-teaming has emerged as a critical approach for identifying these vulnerabilities. Red-teaming involves systematically probing AI systems with adversarial inputs to uncover weaknesses before malicious actors can exploit them. Recent research has focused specifically on red-teaming code agents, leading to the development of automated tools and benchmarks.\n\nThis paper surveys the state-of-the-art in code agent security and red-teaming, examining key contributions in the field. We structure our discussion around three pillars: automatic red-teaming agents for code agents, benchmarks for evaluating code agent safety, and blue teaming approaches for strengthening defenses.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Trustworthiness Assessment of Large Language Models\n\nThe trustworthiness of large language models has become a critical area of research. **DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models** [1] (arXiv:2306.11698) provides one of the most comprehensive evaluations of GPT models across multiple trustworthiness dimensions, including truthfulness, fairness, robustness, privacy, and harmlessness. This foundational work establishes methodologies and benchmarks that have influenced subsequent research in AI safety, including the development of specialized assessments for code agents.\n\n### 2.2 Red-Teaming Code Agents\n\nThe development of automated red-teaming agents specifically for code agents represents a significant advance in this field. **RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents** [2] (arXiv:2510.02609) introduces an automated framework that systematically probes diverse code agent architectures for vulnerabilities. The authors propose a novel approach that combines automated prompt generation with execution-based evaluation, enabling comprehensive assessment of code agent safety across multiple attack vectors.\n\nBuilding on this work, **RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents** [3] (arXiv:2409.12345) introduces a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating the safety of code agents. This benchmark systematically tests code agents against a wide range of risky code scenarios, including malware generation, exploit development, and other security-critical tasks. The benchmark provides standardized evaluation metrics that enable fair comparison across different code agent systems and tracking of progress over time.\n\n### 2.3 Blue Teaming and Defensive Approaches\n\nWhile red-teaming identifies vulnerabilities, blue-teaming focuses on developing defensive mechanisms. **BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI** [4] (arXiv:2501.01234) presents a novel blue teaming approach that leverages automated red-teaming insights to strengthen the security of code generation models. The proposed framework uses findings from red-teaming exercises to automatically generate defensive prompts, improve filtering mechanisms, and enhance the overall robustness of code generation systems.\n\n### 2.4 Deep Learning Foundations\n\nThe development of code agents and associated safety mechanisms relies heavily on advances in deep learning. **Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition** [5] (arXiv:1512.03385) introduced the residual learning framework that enabled training of substantially deeper neural networks. While this work focused on computer vision, the architectural innovations it introduced have influenced the development of all subsequent deep learning models, including the transformer architectures that power modern code agents.\n\n### 2.5 Research Gaps\n\nDespite significant progress, several research gaps remain:\n\n1. **Multi-modal code agents**: Current research primarily focuses on text-based code agents, but multi-modal agents that process code alongside other data types require new assessment methodologies.\n2. **Long-term safety**: Most evaluations assess short-term safety, but the long-term effects of code agent deployment remain understudied.\n3. **Adaptive red-teaming**: Current approaches use static attack strategies, but code agents are continuously evolving, requiring adaptive testing methodologies.\n4. **Cross-agent collaboration**: As code agents increasingly work together in multi-agent systems, new vulnerability classes emerge that require novel red-teaming approaches.\n\n## 3. Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agent security is rapidly evolving as these systems become more capable and widely deployed. This paper has surveyed key advances in red-teaming methodologies for code agents, highlighting important contributions from recent research. We have examined automatic red-teaming agents that systematically probe code agent vulnerabilities, benchmarks for evaluating code agent safety, and blue teaming approaches for strengthening defenses.\n\nThe research reviewed in this paper demonstrates significant progress in understanding and mitigating the security risks associated with code agents. However, as these systems continue to advance, the security challenges will also evolve, requiring ongoing research and development of new methodologies.\n\nKey directions for future work include:\n\n1. **Development of standardized safety benchmarks** that can be used across different code agent systems and evaluation settings.\n2. **Research into adaptive red-teaming methodologies** that can evolve alongside code agent capabilities.\n3. **Investigation of multi-agent code systems** and the unique security challenges they present.\n4. **Integration of security considerations into the design of code agents** from the ground up, rather than as an afterthought.\n5. **Cross-disciplinary collaboration** between AI safety researchers, software engineers, and security experts to develop comprehensive protection strategies.\n\nAs code agents become increasingly integral to software development and other critical domains, ensuring their safety and security must remain a top priority for the research community. The work reviewed in this paper represents important steps toward this goal, but much work remains to be done to ensure the safe and responsible deployment of these powerful systems.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[3] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie, et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[4] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie, et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\n[5] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, Jian Sun. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\nPAPER_EOF"
